Context: before treating the continuous heavy column (§14 ff.), Euler studies a discrete model. The column AB has height 2a and is made of two rigid halves, CA and CB, each of weight M. They are joined at the midpoint C by an elastic hinge (the 'elastrum'). The hinge's restoring moment is Ee times the relative rotation, which is 2θ when each half tilts by θ. The ground reaction at the foot B is BO = 2M (vertical). There is a horizontal reaction A at the top A, a horizontal reaction B at B, and a lateral load C applied at C. The previous page found A = ½C + ½Mθ and B = ½C − ½Mθ.

On this page Euler checks the moment equation about C for the lower arm CB, linearised for small θ:
- the horizontal force B gives moment Ba;
- the reaction 2M, offset aθ from C, gives 2Maθ in the same sense;
- the arm's own weight, offset aθ/2, gives ½Maθ in the opposite sense;
- the spring gives 2Eeθ, also in the opposite sense.
So Ba + 2Maθ = ½Maθ + 2Eeθ. Substituting B gives ½Ca + Maθ = 2Eeθ, the same equation as from the upper arm, which is a consistency check.

§12 solves this as θ = Ca/(4Ee − 2Ma), or conversely C = (2θ/a)(2Ee − Ma). When Ma = 2Ee, a deflection needs no lateral force and θ is indeterminate. Euler reads this as the column bending under its own weight until it collapses. In modern terms this is a static bifurcation (adjacent-equilibrium) criterion for a two-degree-of-freedom rigid-link model of self-weight buckling. On the next page this gives the critical height b = √(8Ee/dd).

The solution can be rewritten as θ = θ0/(1 − Ma/2Ee), where θ0 = Ca/(4Ee) is the deflection with gravity ignored. This is exactly the load-amplification factor 1/(1 − P/Pcr).
